<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">encounters</ansi>

Some rooms in wild and dangerous places can spring a battle on you as you
walk in. There is no command: you step into the room, shapes stir, and a
normal battle begins with your company set up as it is.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">When one can happen</ansi>
  After an ordinary step into a room that allows them, or when a journey
  ends there (<ansi fg="command">help travel</ansi>). Towns, inns, shops and safe rooms do not allow
  them, and nothing rolls for looking around, logging in, or being moved
  by a church or a script. Only the leader's step counts: companions and
  followers moving with you do not roll again.
  Each such entry has a chance: about 15 in 100 by default, and a room can
  set its own, higher or lower or none. <ansi fg="command">scout</ansi> in a room that allows them says
  so and names the zone's level band.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">The zone's level band</ansi>
  <ansi fg="command">look</ansi> and <ansi fg="command">scout</ansi> name the levels a zone's foes come at ("Foes in the Dark
  Forest are of levels 5 to 7") and how that weighs on your company's level
  (the average of you and your companions, rounded), and the web client's
  room panel shows it as "Lv 5-7" beside the zone's name and updates it
  when a level comes: green is easy, yellow fair, orange risky, red
  dangerous.
    easy       your company's level is at or above the band's low end
    fair       one or two levels under it: a fair test
    risky      three or four under: expect losses
    dangerous  five or more under: prepare carefully (formation,
               tactics, healers) or come back stronger

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Breathing room</ansi>
  After any battle, the next two entries into such rooms roll nothing, and
  at least 30 real seconds must pass besides. It is saved with you, so
  crossing a zone, logging out or a restart does not reset it, and a new
  character starts with it. You never face two random groups at once, and
  nothing rolls while you are fighting, travelling or camped.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Who you meet</ansi>
  Two or three foes, now and then four, set by the zone's level band and
  never by your level: a zone for levels 8 to 10 sends foes you can beat at
  those levels, and a company well under the band risks losing. Foes are
  drawn from the zone's own kinds, and a healer is in about one group in
  six and never in a group of four. A company that has set its tactics to
  focus the healer (<ansi fg="command">help tactics</ansi>) cuts such a group down faster. Four foes come from the band's low
  end. How well each fights together follows their level
  (<ansi fg="command">help coordination</ansi>).

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Bosses</ansi>
  A boss waits in a lair and comes with two or three escorts at the band's
  low level. The boss is three levels over them with twice the health,
  and the group fights without a strategy: no focus fire, no guard. What
  makes it frightening is the boss itself, and a hex holds it half as long
  (<ansi fg="command">help hexes</ansi>).
  Beat the boss and its lair falls quiet for your company, and for your
  party, for about half an hour of real time: no boss springs there until
  it has passed, so a lair cannot be farmed. It is saved, so a restart
  does not wake it early. Fleeing or losing starts no wait. While it is
  quiet, <ansi fg="command">look</ansi> and <ansi fg="command">scout</ansi> in the lair say how many minutes are left.
  The wait follows each member who beat the boss, so changing who leads
  the party does not wake it.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Whose fight it is</ansi>
  The foes are set on your company alone. They stand in the room where
  others can see them, but nobody else can start the fight, draw their
  attention, or take their spoils, unless they are in your party or
  alliance and fight beside you (<ansi fg="command">help party</ansi>). If your company flees, is
  beaten, or leaves, the survivors are gone once nobody is fighting them
  any more. They never become a roaming party, and a restart ends them.
  Some enemy groups roam the zone in the open: you can scout, avoid or hunt
  those as you please.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Spoils</ansi>
  Every foe makes its own roll. When the last foe of a group falls, the
  group's cache adds gold by the zone's level band, a chance of gear and
  some goods; a boss's roll gives two pieces of gear (one of them Rare or
  better), three goods rolls and much more gold. Gear is made at the
  zone's item levels. Allied companies each get their own roll, held in a
  corpse claimed by that company (<ansi fg="command">help loot</ansi>), and your battle summary lists
  your spoils (<ansi fg="command">help battle-summary</ansi>).

See also: <ansi fg="command">help combat</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help scout</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help loot</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help rarity</ansi>,
<ansi fg="command">help retreat</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help ambush</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help travel</ansi>,
<ansi fg="command">help gathering</ansi> (hunting draws a group 10% more often).
